  <section class="wrap fiche-body">
    <div>
      <h2>The vibe</h2>
      <p>At the entrance of Saint-Tropez, just past the harbour and its large car park, La Bouillabaisse is a wide sandy beach facing the Maures mountain range — the best spot in town to watch the sunset behind them. Its name comes from a small coastal stream, the ruisseau des Mares, which roughly marks the boundary between Saint-Tropez and the neighbouring town of Gassin, halfway along the beach.</p>

      <h2>What to know before you go</h2>
      <p>Since the July 2026 municipal order, dogs on leash are allowed on Saint-Tropez's town beaches from 6am to 9.30am and 6.30pm to 10pm, from 16 July to 15 September; banned the rest of the day during that period, with more relaxed access off-season. The tourist office's own listing for this beach still shows the generic "dogs not allowed" tag, with no reference to these hours at all — most likely because that listing hasn't been updated since the order, rather than a genuine exception, though that isn't guaranteed. Best to check the signage on site before settling in with your dog.</p>
      <div class="callout">Coco's tip: the Gassin side (west of the ruisseau des Mares) follows that town's own rules, not Saint-Tropez's — stay on the eastern half if you want to be sure which rule applies.</div>

      <h2>Nearby</h2>
      <p>Saint-Tropez's harbour and historic centre are a few minutes' walk away. The coastal path from <a href="/en/fiche-port-grimaud">Port Grimaud</a> reaches La Bouillabaisse after following the whole bay.</p>
